{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "si-01-laws-of-indices",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Laws of Indices",
    "description": "See exponent laws as factor-counting shortcuts, then use them across products, quotients and common-base expressions.",
    "teachingApproach": "Begin with visible repeated factors so every law has a reason, not just a formula. Let one operation change at a time, use trap options built from common exponent mistakes, and end by rewriting different-looking bases to one base before simplifying.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 9, Surds and Indices. Approved scope: Teach the six index laws stated in the chapter as consequences of how many copies of the same factor survive: a^m × a^n = a^(m+n), a^m ÷ a^n = a^(m−n), (a^m)^n = a^(mn), (ab)^n = a^n b^n, (a/b)^n = a^n/b^n, and a^0 = 1 for non-zero a. Start with small powers whose factors can be seen mentally, then move to mixed multiplication/division chains and bases such as 64 and 8 that should first be rewritten to one common base. Include the classic traps of multiplying exponents during ordinary multiplication, subtracting in the wrong direction during division, and treating a^0 as 0. Learners should name the base before touching the exponents and verify a shortcut with one small expanded example."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's make exponents feel less like rules to memorise and more like counting.\n\nWhen the base stays the same, the exponent simply tells us how many copies of that base are present. Once you see those copies, the laws of indices become shortcuts you can trust.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Start with the easiest pattern: multiplication of the same base.\n\n2^3 means 2×2×2.\n2^4 means four more 2s.\n\nTogether there are seven 2s.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "So:\n\n2^3 × 2^4 = 2^(3+4) = 2^7 = 128\n\nThe base stays 2. We add exponents because we are joining factor lists. We do not multiply 3×4.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
